<p>Four things are worth noticing before the lessons take the graph apart:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Most turns call no tool.</strong> A stage's model keeps asking questions until it has everything its tool needs. State changes only when a handler accepts a complete, valid submission.</li>
  <li><strong>A stage change happens inside one turn.</strong> On turns 3, 7, 9, and 13 one node saves its facts and returns <code>go()</code>, and the next node writes the reply. The customer never sees a “moving on” message from the old stage.</li>
  <li><strong>Every price comes from code.</strong> The rating engine priced the tiers on turn 13, and the model presented them from JSON. On turn 14 the model only chose <code>adjust_quote</code>; the numbers and the wording came from code, with no second model call.</li>
  <li><strong>Acceptance is a business event, not a sentence.</strong> Turn 15 completes because <code>accept_quote</code> found <code>selected</code> in the saved tiers. The confirmation, premium, start date, and reference number are all code-owned.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two small inconsistencies are visible too. The model called the middle tier “Selected” on turn 13, while the code's label on turn 14 is “Your selection”. And the confirmation on turn 15 reads “your selected coverage (Your selection)”. <a href="/ezgraph/docs/tutorials/quote-graph/revise-and-accept/">Lesson 9</a> explains both.</p>

